(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-lipid-statins-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Statins & Statin Guidelines Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of these statins is one of the two that can reach high intensity?","choices":["Simvastatin","Pitavastatin","Atorvastatin","Pravastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are the only statins that reach high intensity (50% or greater lowering of LDL, low-density lipoprotein, cholesterol). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A patient on simvastatin develops muscle toxicity. What is required?","choices":["Continue and add a fibrate","Double the dose briefly","Discontinue the statin","Switch to taking it with grapefruit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The presence of muscle toxicity requires discontinuing the statin; myopathy occurs in 0.2 to 0.4% with rare rhabdomyolysis. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is a relative contraindication with statins?","choices":["Erythromycin","Amoxicillin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erythromycin is on the relative contraindication list with cyclosporine and other immunosuppressants, gemfibrozil and niacin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which class is first-line therapy when LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol lowering drugs are indicated?","choices":["Bile acid sequestrants","Fibrates","Statins","Niacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Statins are the most efficacious and best tolerated of all the agents and are first line when LDL-lowering drugs are indicated. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which condition is listed as a contraindication to statins?","choices":["Type 2 diabetes","Hypertension","Prior stroke","Hepatic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic disease and pregnancy are the listed contraindications; liver enzyme rises on therapy are managed by reducing the dose or pausing. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which patient falls into one of the four major statin benefit groups?","choices":["A 55-year-old after a myocardial infarction","A 35-year-old with diabetes and LDL of 120","A 50-year-old with LDL of 150 and 3% risk","A 60-year-old with diabetes and LDL of 60"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clinical atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease is the first benefit group; the others are LDL above 190 mg/dL, diabetes at 40 to 75 with LDL 70 to 189, and high estimated risk. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A patient on verapamil needs a statin. Which statin would CYP3A4 inhibition affect least?","choices":["Atorvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Lovastatin","Simvastatin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rosuvastatin has minimal CYP metabolism, so verapamil's CYP3A4 inhibition matters least; atorvastatin, lovastatin and simvastatin are CYP3A4 substrates. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does inhibiting hepatic cholesterol synthesis lower serum LDL (low-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["It blocks intestinal cholesterol uptake","It binds bile acids in the gut","It activates PPAR-alpha","It upregulates hepatic LDL receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lower intracellular cholesterol stimulates LDL receptor synthesis, increasing hepatic uptake of LDL, VLDL remnants and IDL from the circulation. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which statement about serious liver injury from statins is accurate?","choices":["It affects a quarter of users","It is exceedingly rare","It is the main reason to stop","It occurs regardless of dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Liver enzyme rises occur in 0.5 to 2.5% of patients, dose-dependently, but serious liver problems are exceedingly rare. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which combination is a relative contraindication for statin therapy?","choices":["A statin with ezetimibe","A statin with gemfibrozil","A statin with lisinopril","A statin with amlodipine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Relative contraindications are concomitant cyclosporine or other immunosuppressants, gemfibrozil, niacin and erythromycin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A patient cannot tolerate a moderate- or high-intensity statin. What is recommended?","choices":["Use the maximum tolerated dose","Stop statins permanently","Replace it with niacin alone","Add gemfibrozil to the statin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If high- or moderate-intensity therapy is not tolerated, the maximum tolerated dose is used instead, keeping side effects and interactions in mind. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which is a pleiotropic effect of statins, beyond lowering cholesterol?","choices":["Raised triglycerides","Plaque stabilization","Increased platelet stickiness","Increased inflammatory markers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pleiotropic effects include plaque stabilization, improved endothelial function, platelet inhibition, reduced leukocyte adhesiveness and reduced inflammatory markers. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient's liver enzymes rise after starting atorvastatin. What is the recommended management?","choices":["Stop it and never restart","Continue it at the same dose","Add a fibrate to protect it","Lower the dose or pause it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Liver enzyme rises occur in 0.5 to 2.5% of patients in a dose-dependent manner and are managed by reducing the dose or stopping until levels return to normal. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Under the ACC/AHA (American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association) guideline, why are lipids measured at follow-up visits?","choices":["To titrate to an LDL goal","To detect liver disease early","To assess adherence","To decide whether to add niacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lipids are measured at follow-up to assess adherence to treatment, not to reach a specific LDL (low-density lipoprotein) target. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What happens when LDL (low-density lipoprotein) receptor activity falls?","choices":["LDL accumulates, driving atherosclerosis","HDL accumulates, protecting arteries","Chylomicrons clear faster from blood","Triglycerides fall in the plasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Reduced receptor activity lets LDL particles build up in plasma, which leads to atherosclerosis; most LDL-lowering classes work by raising receptor activity. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
